subproblems.statement:
Let n >= 0 be an integer, let I, L, a, P, Q : R -> R, and let R, M, beta be reals with beta < 1. Suppose that for every t with 0 < t < R:
 (i) I is differentiable at t with I'(t) = P(t);
 (ii) L is continuous at t;
 (iii) I(t) >= 0;
 (iv) n I(t) - t P(t) + t Q(t) = -L(t);
 (v) a(t) = (L(t)/t^(n+1))/(1 + I(t)/t^n);
 (vi) |a(t)| <= M t^(-beta) (real power).
Write int_0^s f for the interval integral of f from 0 to s with respect to Lebesgue measure. Then:
 (A) for every t with 0 < t < R, the function Psi(s) := exp(-int_0^s a)(1 + I(s)/s^n) is differentiable at t with derivative exp(-int_0^t a) Q(t)/t^n;
 (B) for all t, rho with 0 < t <= rho < R: exp(-int_0^rho |a|) <= exp(-int_0^t a) <= exp(int_0^rho |a|).

Let n, l be natural numbers, E := R^{n+l} (Euclidean space), mu a measure on E, y in E, phi : R -> R and S, T, t real numbers. Write r(x) := |x - y|, B_S(y) for the closed ball of radius S about y, and phi' := deriv phi. Assume: T > 0; mu(B_S(y)) < infinity; phi is C^1; phi(u) = 0 for every u >= T; t > 0; and T t < S. Then:
 (a) x -> phi(r(x)/t) is mu-integrable;
 (b) x -> (r(x)/t) phi'(r(x)/t) is mu-integrable;
 (c) the function s -> integral_E phi(r(x)/s) dmu(x) has derivative -(integral_E (r(x)/t) phi'(r(x)/t) dmu(x))/t at s = t.
Integrals are Bochner integrals, and division follows Lean's convention x/0 = 0.
